{{also|abêt|abèt|abét|авет}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰeyd-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|abetten}}, {{m|enm|abette}}, from {{der|en|fro|abeter||to entice}}, from {{m|fro|a-||to}} + {{m|fro|beter||hound on, urge, to bait}}, either from {{der|en|dum|bētan||incite}} or from {{der|en|non|beita||to cause to bite, bait, incite}},<ref>{{R:CDOE|page=2}}</ref> from {{der|en|gem-pro|*baitijaną||to cause to bite}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰeyd-||to split}}. Cognate with {{cog|is|beita||to set dogs on; to feed}}.

Alternate etymology traces the Middle English and Old French words through {{cog|ang|*ābǣtan||to hound on}}, from {{m|ang|ā-}} + {{m|ang|bǣtan||to bait}}, from the same source ({{der|en|gem-pro|*baitijaną}}).

See also {{m|en|bait}}, {{m|en|bet}}.

