===Verb===
{{en-verb|++}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|crime}} To [[incite]]; to [[assist]] or [[encourage]] by aid or [[countenance]] in crime. {{defdate|ca. 1350|1470}}<ref name=SOED>{{R:SOED5|page=4}}</ref>
#: {{syn|en|collude|conspire|connive}}
#: {{co|en|[[aid and abet|aid and '''abet''']]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1823|author=[[w:John Galt (novelist)|John Galt]]|title=Ringan Gilhaize or The Covenanters|url=https://gutenberg.org/files/30749/30749-h/30749-h.htm|passage=Those who would exalt themselves by '''abetting''' the strength of the Godless, and the wrength of the oppressors.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1851|author=Charles G. Davis|title=Report of the Proceedings at the Examination of Charles G. Davis, Esq., on the Charge of Aiding and Abetting in the Rescue of a Fugitive Slave|page=39|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/unitedstatesvsch00davi/page/39/mode/1up|passage=The Statute provides that whoever has been engaged in aiding, '''abetting''', or assisting, ''directly or indirectly'', is criminal.}}
#* {{RQ:Carroll Snark|fit=6|passage=In the matter of Treason the pig would appear / To have aided, but scarcely '''abetted''': / While the charge of Insolvency fails, it is clear, If you grant the plea ‘never indebted.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Michelle Goldberg|title=What Fox News Says When You’re Not Listening|work=The New York Times|date=2023-02-17|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/02/17/opinion/fox-news-dominion.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=The brief, a motion for summary judgment in a case stemming from Fox’s egregiously false claims of Dominion-'''abetted''' election fraud, offers a portrait of extravagant cynicism.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[support]], [[countenance]], [[maintain]], [[uphold]], or [[aid]] (any good cause, opinion, or action). {{defdate|late 16th c.}}<ref name=SOED/>
#: {{syn|en|approve|patronize|sanction|support|tolerate}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=a. 1667|year_published=1835|author=w:Jeremy Taylor|editor=George Rust|title=The whole works of the Right Rev. Jeremy Taylor|passage=Our duty is urged, and our confidence '''abetted'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Thoreau Walden|passage=The elements, however, '''abetted''' me in making a path through the deepest snow in the woods, for when I had once gone through the wind blew the oak leaves into my tracks, where they lodged, and by absorbing the rays of the sun melted the snow, and so not only made a dry bed for my feet, but in the night their dark line was my guide.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/magazine/archive/1952/05/i-like-to-be-a-stranger/640931/|title=I Like to Be a Stranger|year=1952|month=May|author=w:George Santayana|work=The Atlantic|passage=Later some of these artistic friends{{...|—Geoffrey Scott, for instance, in Florence—}}'''abetted''' this ecclesiastical view in so far as they renounced pre-Raphaelism and learned to love the baroque; but that was an aesthetic fashion also, and corrupt,{{...}}}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=September 27, 2017|author=David Browne|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20170928073133/https://www.rollingstone.com/culture/news/hugh-hefner-playboy-founder-dead-at-91-w443922|title=Hugh Hefner, 'Playboy' Founder, Dead at 91|work=Rolling Stone|passage=By the early Seventies, Playboy was selling seven million copies a month and Hefner's globe-trotting lifestyle was '''abetted''' by his private jet, the Big Bunny, that contained a circular bed, an inside disco and a wet bar.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|transitive}} To [[urge]] on, [[stimulate]] (a person to do) something desirable. {{defdate|late 14th|early 17th c.}}<ref name=Chamber>{{R:CDOE|page=6}}</ref>
#: {{syn|en|instigate|foment|encourage|support|Thesaurus:incite}}
#: {{ant|en|baffle|confound|counteract|denounce|deter|Thesaurus:hinder}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To back up one's forecast of a doubtful issue, by staking money, etc., to [[bet]].

